A unary `*` binds looser than a postfix access -- in sizeof too.

THIS IS WHY EVERY BINARY mc-tcc LINKED SEGFAULTED, and it is one line of
precedence in a place nobody was looking.

    sizeof(*p)          on a plain pointer      CORRECT, and always was
    sizeof(*s->tab)     through a member        returned the POINTER's width

`sizeof(*s->tab)` is `sizeof(*(s->tab))`. The stars were applied to the BASE
VARIABLE's type before the member chain was walked, so it measured `(*s).tab`
instead: it stepped State* down to State, looked `tab` up in that, and reported
8 rather than the 16 of the struct pointed at. The two forms disagree only when
a member sits between the star and the name, which is why a plain pointer
tested fine every round.

EXPERIMENT-zz-entry-20 fixed exactly this precedence for the ordinary
expression path, in postfix_expr, by deferring the dereference past the postfix
chain. sizeof kept its own copy of the rule and never learned it. That is the
tenth "one rule, several implementations, and the copies disagree" in this
series and the most expensive so far.

WHAT IT COST. tccelf.c:815 sizes the symbol-attribute table with

    tab = tcc_realloc(s1->sym_attrs, n * sizeof(*s1->sym_attrs));
    memset(s1->sym_attrs + s1->nb_sym_attrs, 0,
           (n - s1->nb_sym_attrs) * sizeof(*s1->sym_attrs));

so the table was ALLOCATED at half the width its own indexing strides through.
The first entries sit inside it and the later ones read whatever follows on the
heap. Measured on tests2/00_assignment, linking the same object two ways, with
a probe printing every input to the GOT relocation:

    sym_index    control      mc-tcc
        2        0x18         0x18
        3        0x20         0x65007374     <-- printable ASCII, from a string
        4        0x28         0x20

got_sh_addr, addr and sym_index agree exactly; only got_offset is wrong, and
the sequence shifts down a slot after the bad one. One GOT entry therefore
resolved to 0x654281f0 instead of 0x420ea0 -- an ADRP page offset of 0x65028000
against 0x20000 -- and the linked program died on its second string literal.

HOW IT WAS FOUND, because five earlier attempts at this fault were wrong. The
move that worked was not another probe or another bisection; it was splitting
tcc's own phases and cross-linking against the control:

    mc-tcc -E     rc=0        preprocessing is fine
    mc-tcc -c     rc=0        CODE GENERATION IS FINE
    mc-tcc -c, control links  42 / 64 / 12, 34 -- correct output
    mc-tcc -c, mc-tcc links   42, then SIGSEGV

That put the whole fault in the linker and cleared codegen in one command,
after which byte-diffing the two binaries found a single ADRP+LDR pair and the
probe named the one input that differed. Bisecting the SOURCE had produced two
confident wrong answers first -- "a declaration after a statement" and "two
printf calls" -- because both were really measuring heap layout.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT COVER. `sizeof(*x[0])` and `sizeof(**s->tab)` take the same
path and are now right for the same reason, but a dereference applied to a CALL
result inside sizeof is untested; nothing in tcc writes one.

    difftest        91 pass / 0 fail amd64, 93 / 0 aarch64, no known gaps
    the twelve      12 / 12
    stage-2 corpus  419 / 426, unchanged
    tests2          pass 56, fail 1, n/a 72   -- was pass 0
    layout sweep    16 / 16 filename lengths on the 00_assignment shape,
                    where every length failed before; the layout dependence
                    is gone rather than moved, which is the actual proof

